= Grade by grade

Each grade band from VB to V8+ gets a guide in the same six parts, from what problems look like to why people stall. Read the guide for your grade and the one above it. If you climb most V4s, read the V4 and V5 guides. If you are stuck between two grades, @cons-plateau helps you find out why.

*Indoor grades are local.* #ix("Grades!indoor variation") The same grade can feel different in another gym, and many walls grade by #jt("circuit") (a colour that spans two or three grades) #src("F26"). #ix("Circuits") @grades-indoor explains why. So treat the guides as a picture of what is typical, not as a test. #link(<grade-conversion>)[The grade conversion appendix] converts V-grades to #jt("Font grade", body: [Font grades]) (the European scale).

*The badges.* Each band lists every technique whose Part II badge, such as #badge(4), falls at that grade. A badge is this book's estimate of where a technique starts to matter, because no coaching scheme ties named techniques to V-grades #src("F28", "F32"). Two kinds of source come close. Gyms run technique clinics for named grade bands #src("F33", "F34", "F35"). France's climbing federation lists the skills it teaches at each of eight levels. It names a boulder grade for its fifth, sixth and seventh levels: Font 5c, 6A and 6B, or about V2, V3 and V4 #src("F60", "F61"). It lists what climbers learn at a level, which is often earlier than the grade where a technique starts to matter.

*The physical side.* The max-hang numbers are averages across men and women, from a large database of tested climbers #src("F1"). Climbers at the same grade differ widely. The descriptions under "What problems look like" are this book's picture of a typical gym, not measurements.

#notebox[How long climbers have been climbing][
#ix("Progression!years climbing")
Lattice Training#ix("Lattice Training") holds data on about 65,000 climbers. Climbers at V1–V4 report 5.8 years of climbing on average. The average rises to 8.6 years at V5–V7 and 9.7 years at V8–V11 #src("F2"). Lattice's author suspects that casual long-time climbers inflate the V1–V4 figure, and calls that a hunch without data.

Older climbers at a grade report more years of climbing.#ix("Age!years climbing") At V8, climbers under 25 report 3.8 years on average. Climbers over 40 report 13.0 years #src("F2").

Haven't climbed a V5 after a year? These figures give you no reason to think that's slow. All of them are self-reported. Lattice, a training company, reports how long climbers at a grade have been climbing, not how long the grade took. One forum thread does ask what grade people reached after a year. Its answers range from about V4 outdoors to V8 indoors, but the posters are self-selected #src("F47").
]

== How gyms grade <grades-indoor>

#ix("Grades!indoor", main: true)
A grade tells you how hard a problem is meant to be. Indoors it is a useful guide but a poor measure.#ix("Grades!how gyms grade") The #jt("setter") (the person who designs a problem) usually suggests a grade after a try or two and may adjust it after asking other setters or climbers #src("E58"). In some gyms the setters #jt("forerun") (test-climb) each other's problems and agree a grade #src("E50"). Either way, setters grade a problem against the others in the same gym #src("F27"). One setter says what matters most is consistency: a gym can grade hard or easy, as long as its grades stay the same as problems come and go. Regulars then adapt to that gym #src("E46").

#lead-label[Why grades differ between gyms.]

- *The spread is large.* An analysis of 40 US gyms#ix("Studies!grades in 40 US gyms") found grades differing by two to four V-grades between gyms. Its author, the founder of a gym-listing website, reckons a solid V4 climber could expect anything from V2 to V6 #src("E53"). We found no peer-reviewed study of indoor grades.
- *Gym type and walls.* Large commercial chains graded about one V-grade #jt("soft", body: [softer]) (easier for the number) than gyms focused on training. Steep gyms graded harder, while gyms with lots of slab or competition-style setting graded softer #src("E53"). Gyms with many beginners tend to soften the lower grades instead of splitting them #src("E57").
- *Style and your body.* Slab, overhang and dynamic problems test different skills, and one awkward move can make a whole problem feel harder #src("E54"). Height, reach, finger size and style all change how a grade feels #src("E57", "E51", "E46").

#lead-label[Calibrating yourself in a new gym.#ix("Grades!in a new gym")]

+ Ask the staff how their system works and how it compares with standard grades #src("E55", "E56"). #link(<grade-conversion>)[The grade conversion appendix] covers colours and circuits.
+ Warm up three or four grades below your maximum. Start below your limit while you learn the gym's style #src("E53", "E56").
+ Pick one reference problem at your home gym for each style: steep overhang, small edges, slab, jumps and squeezing. After three or four sessions, compare the new gym's problems with them #src("E53").
+ Keep track of which problems you climb reliably and which feel harder or easier for your body #src("E51").

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ VB–V0

== VB–V0 (Font~3–\u{2060}4+) <grade-0>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at VB–V0][
  + Watch each foot onto its hold before you weight it (#tref("precise-footwork", body: [precise footwork], show-page: false)).
  + Pause on #tref("straight-arms", body: [straight arms], show-page: false) on an overhang and stand tall on a vertical wall.
  + Stand up on your legs; let your arms only steer.
  + Say which hand goes to each hold before you start.
  + Jump off from low down and land with bent knees.
]

==== What problems look like

The wall is vertical, a gentle #tref("slab", body: [slab]) (leaning back) or a slight #tref("overhang", body: [overhang]). The holds are #tref("jug", body: [jugs]) and deep edges you can wrap your fingers over. Each foothold takes your whole forefoot. Problems have four to eight moves, laid out like a ladder with a foothold under each reach. No move needs momentum, so you can stop and hang anywhere.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Three habits carry you through this band and every band after it. Place each foot exactly (#tref("precise-footwork", inner: true)). When you pause on an overhang, hang on #tref("straight-arms", body: [straight arms]) with your shoulders set; on a vertical wall, stand tall. Move up by standing on your feet, not by pulling with your arms. Learn to jump off and land too#ix("Falling!practice") (@cons-falling): NICAS and the DAV#ix("DAV") both start their schemes with how to boulder safely #src("F28", "F32").

#grade-techniques(0)

==== Focus

- Look at each foothold until the inside edge of your big toe is on the best part of it. Then put your weight on it.
- Climb many different problems each session. One Lattice post advises 6–12 months of climbing before fingerboard training#ix("Fingerboard!when to start") #src("F6"). Coaches differ on gentle hangs (@cons-adult).
- Learn how your gym marks problems: which holds are the start, how to finish and which colour is which grade #src("F26").

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Find the start holds (often one marked hold per hand) and the finish hold. Then count the hand moves and say which hand goes to each hold.
- Point out the foothold for each reach. If a foothold sits right under your next handhold, step up onto it before you reach.

==== Physical side

Nothing specific. Any healthy adult has the strength for this band.

==== Why people stall here

- *Bent arms.* You get #jt("pump", body: [pumped]) (tired, swollen forearms) after a few moves, and coaches put it down to bent elbows. Fix (coaching advice): pause before every reach. Straighten your arms on an overhang and stand tall on a vertical wall.
- *Noisy feet.* Your shoes scuff and slip off. Fix: climb whole problems placing every foot without a sound.
- *Fear of falling.* Fix: practise falling from low down, a little higher each session (@cons-falling).

One blog says most people get through this band in their first few sessions #src("F48").

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V1

== V1 (Font 5) <grade-1>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V1][
  + Shift your hips over the pushing foot before every reach.
  + Stand on the inside #tref("edging", body: [edge], show-page: false) facing in, the outside edge when turned.
  + Press the rubber flat on the wall where no foothold exists (#tref("smearing", body: [smearing], show-page: false)).
  + Pull each hold away from the way it faces.
  + Grip each hold only as hard as it needs.
]

==== What problems look like

Walls are mostly vertical or slightly overhanging. Jugs give way to #jt("incut") edges, whose lip curls back towards you. You also meet your first holds to pull sideways. Footholds are smaller and sometimes off to the side, so more moves depend on a weight shift before the reach. Moves are still #jt("static"): you could stop at any point. Problems have five to eight moves.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Use #tref("edging", body: [edging]) on the outside of the shoe as well as the inside. Use #tref("smearing", body: [smearing]) where there is no foothold. Pull a #tref("sidepull", body: [sidepull]) towards you. A #tref("high-step", body: [high step]) needs a #tref("rockover", body: [rockover]) to finish it: move your hips across the high foot, then stand up. Many V1s ask you to bring both hands onto one hold (#tref("matching", inner: true)). Others ask you to turn both knees out so your hips come in (#tref("frog", inner: true)). In a corner or between two #tref("volume", body: [volumes]) (big bolted-on shapes), push your feet out against both sides (#tref("stemming", inner: true)).

#grade-techniques(1)

==== Focus

Before each reach, shift your hips sideways over the foot you will push from. Grip no harder than you need. On easy problems, relax your fingers until they start to slip, then add a little.

==== Reading problems at this grade

Read which way each handhold faces. A hold that faces left puts your body to its right (Chapter 1). Then look for footholds that bring your hips under the next handhold.

==== Physical side

Nothing specific.

==== Why people stall here

- *Square-on climbing.* You keep your chest to the wall and stand on both toe tips for every move, so sideways reaches fall short. Fix: square on suits most vertical walls, so on easy problems that lean towards you, practise turning one hip into the wall (#tref("hip-turn", inner: true)).
- *Over-gripping.* Fix: the loose-grip habit above.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V2

== V2 (Font 5+) <grade-2>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V2][
  + Turn one hip into the wall on steep ground (#tref("hip-turn", body: [hip turn], show-page: false)).
  + Put out a #tref("flag", body: [flag], show-page: false) before a hand lets go.
  + Lean off two sideways holds that face the same way (#tref("layback", body: [layback], show-page: false)).
  + Bring your feet high under an #tref("undercling", body: [undercling], show-page: false) before you reach.
  + On the steep, hang on straight arms so your biceps can rest.
]

==== What problems look like

Gentle overhangs of about 10–25° become common. Holds include ones you pull sideways or pull up from below. Some moves only work with your body turned sideways to the wall. Footholds no longer sit under every handhold, so you often reach from one foot while the other has nothing useful. Problems have five to ten moves. A short #jt("dynamic") move (one that uses momentum, so you can't stop halfway) may appear, but most V2s still go statically.

==== Techniques that start to matter

On the steep, learn to turn a hip in (#tref("hip-turn", inner: true)); the #tref("backstep", body: [backstep]) builds on this from V3. The #tref("flag", body: [side flag]) stops the #tref("barn-door", body: [barn door]) swing when one hand lets go. Two sideways holds facing the same way ask for a #tref("layback", body: [layback]). An #tref("undercling", body: [undercling]) at chest height asks you to bring your feet high. Some moves, especially on #tref("traverse", body: [traverses]), ask you to swap feet on one hold (#tref("foot-swap", inner: true)). On #jt("crimp", body: [crimps]) (small edges held on the fingertips), make the #tref("half-crimp", body: [half crimp]) your default from the start, so you don't fall into the habit of full crimping. As footholds shrink, #tref("slab") starts to matter. Problems that finish on top ask for a #tref("mantle", body: [mantle]) and a #tref("top-out", body: [top-out]).

A forum poster says some V2s can still be pulled on muscle #src("F44"). Avoid building that habit. On big holds low on the wall, start practising a partial #tref("drop-knee", body: [drop knee]), small #tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoints]) and the #tref("dyno", body: [dyno]).

#grade-techniques(2)

==== Focus

- Learn to tell front-on moves from side-on moves and to switch between them on purpose. On an overhang, put the outside edge of your shoe on the foothold and turn that hip into the wall. The shoulder on that side rises, which lengthens your reach.
- Press the opposite foot into the wall as a flag *before* one hand lets go.

==== Reading problems at this grade

Two holds facing the same way, such as two sideways holds facing left, mean a layback. A single foothold far out to one side, with nothing on the other side, means a flag. From V3, either may mean a backstep instead.

==== Physical side

Nothing specific.

==== Why people stall here

- *Barn-dooring.* You swing off when one hand releases. Fix: the flag above, *before* the hand moves.
- *Arms tire on the steep.* Fix: straighten your arms and turn a hip in, so your biceps can rest.

#ix("Plateau!at V2") Many climbers stall here: one gym runs a clinic for those who struggle to reach V2 #src("F36"). In one small study, a few beginners improved suddenly once they found side-on positions #src("G45").

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V3

== V3 (Font 6A/6A+) <grade-3>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V3][
  + Catch poor holds at the top of your rise (#tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoint], show-page: false)).
  + Hook a heel on hip-high holds to pull your hips in (#tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hook], show-page: false)).
  + Drive your toes into the wall to keep your feet on overhangs.
  + Sink your hips, then drive with your legs to create momentum.
  + Spend one session in four on the style you avoid, slab or steep.
]

==== What problems look like

V3s come in two families. *Steep* problems sit on 20–40° overhangs with mid-sized edges and rounded holds. Your feet stay on only if you pull with them. *Slab and vertical* problems have blank wall, #tref("volume", body: [volumes]) and small edges for the feet. There, balance is the #jt("crux") (the hardest move). Many V3s are built around one clear move, such as a long span or a hook with the heel. Problems have four to ten moves.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Learn the #tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoint]): catch the hold at the top of a small upward movement, when you have stopped rising and feel lighter. Use your first #tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hook]) on a good hold to pull your hips in. Learn #tref("overhang", body: [overhang]) basics: #tref("toe-pull") and #tref("body-tension") keep your feet on, while a #tref("backstep", body: [backstep]) or a partial #tref("drop-knee", body: [drop knee]) turns a hip to the wall. Where you can't turn in, a #tref("lock-off", body: [lock-off]) holds you for a slow reach. Your hands meet the #tref("sloper", body: [sloper]), the #tref("pinch", body: [pinch]) and the #tref("gaston", body: [gaston]), a hold you push outwards with your elbow out. On big holds, start practising the #tref("lunge", body: [one-handed dyno]), the #tref("double-dyno", body: [double dyno]) and the #tref("run-and-jump", body: [run-and-jump]).

#grade-techniques(3)

==== Focus

- *Toes into the wall on steep ground.* Push through the front of the shoe into the hold, not down along the wall #src("F41").
- *Create momentum.* Sink your hips a little, then drive up with your legs. Your arms guide the movement rather than haul it #src("F33", "F39").
- *Flash more.* Read each V3 fully before you touch it, then try to #jt("flash") it: climb it first go, without falling.

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Can you catch the crux hold while moving, or must you arrive static (#tref("lock-off", inner: true))? You can catch a deep hold with a lip. A poor sloper needs a deadpoint or a slow reach.
- Is there a foothold at hip height on the side you move towards? It may be meant for a heel hook.
- On slab, find where you can stand, then blank wall clean enough to #tref("smearing", body: [smear]) on.
- On a pinch, find the thumb side before you set off.

// these two go over the page together, not the last alone
#keep-together[
==== Physical side

Lattice tests a #jt("max hang")#ix("Max hang!averages by grade"): the most extra weight you can hang for a few seconds. In its lowest band, V1–V4, climbers add about 19~kg on two arms #src("F1"). That figure mixes men and women. The women in the band average about 7~kg #src("F1"), so many V3 climbers climb well on far less than 19~kg.

==== Why people stall here

- *Feet cutting on the steep.* Fix: keep both feet pressing into the wall, even the one you are about to move, and keep your hips close #src("F41").
- *Climbing one style.*#ix("Plateau!climbing one style") You climb all the steep V3s and avoid the slabs, or the reverse. Lattice's coaches see plateaus#ix("Plateau!when it happens") "somewhere between V3–V6", usually in the first couple of years. They list avoiding weak styles as a main cause #src("F9"). Fix: spend one session in four on the style you avoid.
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V4

== V4 (Font 6B/6B+) <grade-4>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V4][
  + Start big steep moves from straight arms, hips back and down.
  + Pull with your #tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hook], show-page: false): bend the knee and drag your hips in.
  + Use a #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hook], show-page: false) to stop the swing when a hand lets go.
  + Keep both toes pressing in when your hands reach far (#tref("body-tension", body: [body tension], show-page: false)).
  + Plan your body position for the hardest move before you start.
]

==== What problems look like

Expect overhangs of 30–45°, or vertical walls with poor feet. #jt("crimp", body: [Crimps]) and rounded holds replace most jugs. Footholds are small edges or the faces of #tref("volume", body: [volumes]). A V4 often combines two or three techniques, e.g. a heel hook that sets up a long reach. One or two moves need momentum, e.g. a jump to a big hold or a #jt("coordination move") (a timed sequence of jumps or swings). Problems have four to eight hard moves.

==== Techniques that start to matter

The #tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hook]) becomes an active pull: bend the knee and pull with the #jt("hamstring") (the muscle at the back of the thigh) to draw your hips in. Add the #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hook]) to stop a swing and to keep your feet on a #tref("roof", body: [roof]). Squeezing between two holds becomes a technique of its own (#tref("compression", inner: true)). Gyms start to set the #tref("lunge", body: [one-handed dyno]) and the #tref("dyno", body: [dyno]) here, so you need #tref("latching", body: [latching]) to hold the catch and #tref("cutting-loose", body: [cutting loose]) to control the swing. The #tref("run-and-jump", body: [run-and-jump]) appears on problems set in a competition style. Your hands meet the #tref("pocket", body: [pocket]). On rounded holds the thumb starts to help (#tref("thumb-catch", inner: true)). By now, momentum is a normal tool, not a last resort.

#grade-techniques(4)

==== Focus

- *Choose #jt("static") or #jt("dynamic").* For big moves on overhangs, start with straight arms and your hips back and down, then drive from your feet. Newer climbers tend to #tref("lock-off", body: [lock off]) too much #src("F39").
- *Body tension.* When your hands are far above you or out to the side, keep pushing through both toes. If your feet go light, pull on the footholds with your toes.
- *Repeat, then reach up.* Repeat V4s until they feel smooth. Put real attempts into at least one V5 every session. Lattice counts learning chances, not #jt("send", body: [sends]) (clean climbs of a problem) #src("F9").

==== Reading problems at this grade

Find the #jt("crux") first, then work backwards. What body position must you be in to start the crux move? Next, find the move where your feet will swing off and decide how you will catch the swing before you leave the ground.

#lead-label[Look for]
- heel-hook holds: a horizontal edge or volume at hip height, out to the side.
- toe-hook features: a hold or volume edge that faces down or sideways at foot level.

==== Physical side

#ix("Finger strength!by grade") Lattice's V1–V4 band adds about 19~kg on a two-arm #jt("max hang") #src("F1").

==== Why people stall here

#ix("Plateau!V4 to V5", main: true)
Many climbers stall between V4 and V5: the plateau is common enough that gyms name clinics after it #src("F33", "F35").

- *Strength instead of position.* Lattice's#ix("Lattice Training") coaches say strong climbers learn technique more slowly, because they can muscle through moves #src("F9"). After each fall, ask: "If I'm strong enough for this, what am I missing?" #src("F42")
- *Locking off on the steep.* Newer climbers hold bent-arm #tref("lock-off", body: [lock-offs]) too often. Start big moves from straight arms instead, hips back and down. Press into the foot rather than pulling the knee in tight #src("F39"). See #tref("pre-loading").
- *Staying tight to the wall when you need to turn.* Pinned to the wall, you can't rotate, so you swing when a hand comes off. Before you let go with the lower hand, relax away from it. Find the point where you balance, then move #src("F40").
- *Ignoring the off foot.* The #jt("off foot"), the foot not carrying you, controls rotation. Push it into the wall at roughly right angles to the line from your hand to your foot. Touch it briefly on the wall to stop a swing #src("F41"). #ix("Off foot") See #tref("barn-door").
- *Avoiding weak styles and limit attempts.*#ix("Plateau!climbing one style") Lattice lists both among the main causes of plateaus #src("F9").
- *Missing a few techniques.* Gym clinics for the "V5 plateau" teach a short list: #tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hooks]), toe hooks, #tref("flag", body: [flagging]), #tref("body-tension", body: [body tension]), #tref("compression", body: [compression]), deadpoints and sequencing #src("F35"). A V5–V6 clinic adds #tref("drop-knee", body: [drop knees]), #tref("mantle", body: [mantles]), #tref("rockover", body: [rockovers]) and dynos #src("F34").

@cons-plateau helps you find out which of these stops you.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V5

== V5 (Font 6C/6C+) <grade-5>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V5][
  + Place each foot before you move a hand.
  + Find the one technique the hardest move demands, then do it exactly.
  + Climb V5s in every style before you chase V6.
  + Give each new V5 one real first-go attempt before you work it.
  + Be ready for the odd two-handed or footless move: the #tref("double-dyno", body: [double dyno], show-page: false) and #tref("campus-move", body: [campus~move], show-page: false).
]

==== What problems look like

Expect steep walls with small #jt("crimp", body: [crimps]), poor rounded holds and pinches. Footholds are poor or missing and rests are few.

#lead-in[The table compares a typical V5 with a typical V4. In it, #jt("beta") is the sequence of moves that works. The rows come from gym clinics, one head setter's account of a gym's circuits and forum reports, not from measurement. The forum reports are about the step to V6, which shows where V5 is heading.]

#gtable(
  columns: (28mm, 1fr, 1fr),
  table.header(th[], th[Typical V4], th[Typical V5]),
  [*Holds*], [Edges and rounded holds with some lip; a jug to rest on], [Smaller #jt("crimp", body: [crimps]), rounder holds, pinches; few rests #src("F48", "F46")],
  [*Wall angle*], [30–45°, or vertical with poor feet], [Often steeper: forum climbers find the harder problems more overhung #src("F46")],
  [*Footholds*], [Small edges, volumes], [Smaller edges or none; tension holds the feet on #src("F48")\; feet often swing off],
  [*The #jt("crux")*], [Several solutions work], [More often a test of one skill, such as a #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hook]) to stop a swing. One head setter says a gym's problems switch from teaching skills to testing them in its V4–V6 circuit #src("E52")],
  [*Dynamic moves*], [Deadpoints, lunges, the odd dyno or coordination move #src("F60", "E52")], [Committed #tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoints]), #tref("dyno", body: [dynos]) and coordination moves such as #tref("run-and-jump", body: [run-and-jump]) #src("F34")],
  [*Body tension*], [Needed on steep moves #src("F60")], [Held for longer, through slow moves #src("F65"), to keep the feet on while a hand moves #src("F35", "F48")],
  [*Complexity*], [More than at V2–V3: the same setter starts "surprising" climbers with complexity and risk in the V2–V4 circuit #src("E52")], [More again, and sustained: the V4–V6 circuit adds sustained intensity #src("E52")],
  [*How it goes*], [First go, or in one session], [Several sessions, since it is your next grade. You work out the beta move by move],
)

==== Techniques that start to matter

Some moves take your feet or both hands off the wall: the #tref("double-dyno", body: [double dyno]), the #tref("campus-move", body: [campus move]) and #tref("foot-catch", body: [jumping to a foothold]). Steep hooking extends to the #tref("bicycle", body: [bicycle]), the #tref("heel-toe-cam", body: [heel–toe cam]) and, where a volume or corner allows it, the #tref("knee-bar", body: [knee bar]). The tiniest edges may need a #tref("full-crimp", body: [full crimp]), so keep it for those moves. Some slabs turn dynamic (#tref("dynamic-slab", inner: true)). #jt("comp style", body: [Comp-style]) problems (set like competition problems) add the #tref("pogo", body: [pogo]) and #tref("comp-moves", body: [competition set pieces]) such as the tic-tac, the paddle dyno and the double clutch.

#grade-techniques(5)

==== Focus

- *Build a V5 base.* Once your first V5s go, climb many V5s in different styles until the grade stops depending on style. Read each new one fully, then give it one real #jt("flash") attempt before you work it.
- *Finish the technique list.* Practise the V5–V6 clinic techniques on V4s first #src("F34").

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Decide where each foot goes *before* each hand move, because at V5 the feet decide whether the hand move works. Before you start, find the hidden footholds, such as a volume's edge or a hold's side.
- Find the crux by its holds: the smallest hold, the longest gap or the hold facing the "wrong" way. Then ask what the crux hold wants. If you pull it from below, you can do it statically. A #tref("sidepull", body: [sidepull]) or #tref("gaston", body: [gaston]) needs a body position, and a sloper wants low hips and straight arms.
- Find the swing move, where a hand lets go and the feet are poor. Decide in advance: flag, toe hook, or let the feet swing and catch them.
- Read in chunks (@reading-method). A Lattice summary ties previews of two to four holds to difficult routes, which doesn't show that they help #src("F11").

==== Physical side

Lattice's V5–V7 band adds about 24~kg on a two-arm #jt("max hang"), against about 19~kg in its V1–V4 band #src("F1"). The bands are too wide to show the step from V4 to V5. @cons-plateau covers the choice between technique and strength.

==== Why people stall here

- *Staying on the V5s that suit you.* It feels like progress, but your weak styles stay weak.
- *V6s feel like a different sport.* One forum climber found V6s "too overhung and crimpy or too shouldery". Replies pointed to overhang technique and finger strength for small holds. Others said each gym's setting has blind spots, so visit other gyms #src("F46").

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V6

== V6 (Font 7A) <grade-6>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V6][
  + Climb the style you avoid, usually steep walls with small edges.
  + Use #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hooks], show-page: false) and heel hooks as main holds, not extras.
  + Stop the swing and replace your feet without letting go (#tref("cutting-loose", body: [cutting loose], show-page: false)).
  + Squeeze with both arms and both feet on #tref("compression", body: [compression], show-page: false) problems.
  + Visit other gyms to find the problem types you lack.
]

==== What problems look like

Expect walls of 40–50°, roofs, or very technical vertical faces. Holds are small #jt("crimp", body: [crimps]), poor rounded holds and wide pinches. Some problems make you squeeze between two holds or volumes. Expect one or two big dynamic moves, or long runs of medium-hard moves.

==== Techniques that start to matter

This book's catalogue has nothing new for this band, so the work is on the techniques you already know. Hooks become main holds, not just aids. #tref("compression") uses both arms and both feet. #tref("cutting-loose", body: [Cutting loose]) becomes routine: you stop the swing and put your feet back on without losing your hands. #jt("comp style", body: [Comp-style]) problems keep using the #tref("comp-moves", body: [competition set pieces]) from V5.

#grade-techniques(6)

==== Focus

- Work on your weak styles, usually overhangs and small holds. Climb in other gyms to find them #src("F46").
- Pick #jt("project", body: [projects]) (problems you work over several sessions) and stay with them.
- Be precise about where each finger and toe goes #src("F38").

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Find the places where the feet are low and far from the hands. Those need the most tension.
- Find the hooking features and the target hold of each dynamic move.
- Read the texture of each rounded hold and which way it grips best.

==== Physical side

One head setter says a gym's problems start to test finger strength from V6 #src("E52"). Forum climbers recommend finger training to break into V6 #src("F46"). One coaching company puts the change later: up to V7 or V8, it says, most problems need only moderate finger strength #src("F64").

==== Why people stall here

- *Climbing V6 like V5.* Solutions that worked at V5 are too loose here.
- *Holds too small to use.* Your fingers can't yet hold the small crimps.
- *A comfortable home gym.* It hides your weakest style. Fix: the focus points above.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V7

== V7 (Font 7A+) <grade-7>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V7][
  + Test for your weaknesses instead of guessing them.
  + Where a move needs it, catch the hold fast and exactly as you arrive (#tref("latching", body: [latching], show-page: false)).
  + Try two or three foot options for each hard move.
  + Decide which way to turn your hand on every poor hold.
  + Expect your hardest problems to take many sessions: one coach writes of climbers spending season after season on theirs #src("E81").
]

==== What problems look like

The terrain is the same as V6 with less margin. Holds are a size smaller and feet are worse. Moves are longer or more powerful. Many climbers at this level measure themselves on #jt("training board", body: [training boards]) such as the MoonBoard, Kilter and Tension: standard steep walls with a fixed grid of holds.

==== Techniques that start to matter

This book's catalogue has nothing new for this band. What changes is how exactly you do the ones you know. Compression must hold for longer. Toe and heel hooks must grip poor features. You must #tref("latching", body: [latch]) holds fast and accurately at the end of a dynamic move. Lattice calls this #jt("contact strength"): catching a hold quickly as you arrive #src("F14"). #ix("Contact strength") Save the fastest catches on small holds for moves that need them, because a very fast grab can rupture a #jt("pulley") (a band that holds a finger tendon against the bone) #src("C25").

#grade-techniques(7)

==== Focus

Find your weaknesses by testing them, not by guessing #src("F14"). Choose #jt("project", body: [projects]) that take weeks.

==== Reading problems at this grade

Work out fine #jt("beta"): decide which way to turn a hand on a hold and exactly where a toe goes. Try two or three foot options for each hard hand move before you commit to one.

==== Physical side

Men at V7–V8 average about 145% of bodyweight on Lattice's two-rep weighted pull-up test #src("F5").

==== Why people stall here

Some climbers test strong on a #jt("hangboard") (a board of edges to hang from) but climb below that level. Lattice says they may lack contact strength, or the power for several hard moves in a row #src("F14").

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V8+

== V8 and above (Font~7B~and~up) <grade-8>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V8 and above][
  + Work out sequences for your own body, not an average one.
  + Build strength on the exact grips your hardest problems use.
  + Plan rests and attempts for every session on a hard problem.
  + Expect each new grade to take longer than the last: in Lattice's data each grade band goes with more years of climbing #src("F2").
  + Keep placing your feet as precisely as you did at V0.
]

==== What problems look like

Expect hard versions of everything above: the steepest walls, and holds that only work in one exact position. One head setter says a gym's hardest circuits test finger strength, complex sequences and risky coordination moves #src("E52"). One coach says harder mostly means bigger moves: to fairly good holds on #jt("training board", body: [training boards]), and to more sloping holds on gym problems #src("F65").

==== Techniques that start to matter

This book's catalogue has nothing new for this band. Progress comes from strength on specific grips and from #jt("beta") worked out for your body.

#grade-techniques(8)

==== Focus

Build strength for specific grips, pinches and squeezing. Refine your tactics.

==== Reading problems at this grade

Your build drives the solutions. Read for *your* body, not an average one.

// these two go over the page together, as on V6 and V7, not the last alone
#keep-together[
==== Physical side

Lattice's V8–V11 band adds about 38~kg on a two-arm #jt("max hang") #src("F1"). Climbers in that band train 4.4 hours a week #src("F3"). Above roughly 160–165% of bodyweight on the weighted pull-up test, Lattice sees diminishing returns from more pulling strength in men #src("F5").

==== Why people stall here

The data doesn't show what holds people here. Climbers at this level report about ten years of climbing on average, more if they are older (see the chapter's opening box). Lattice, which sells training plans, reports a 51-year-old client sending V8 on one #src("F13") (see @cons-adult).
]

